00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:03.320
All right, let's get started.

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:09.100
Any organization needs to
decide their cloud strategy

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:11.140
based on multiple
paths that they can

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:13.870
take on moving to the cloud.

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:18.490
Organizations can decide to
lift and shift their workloads

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:21.670
into the cloud and
then modernize it,

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:25.510
or they can decide
to modernize on prem

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:28.480
and then move to the cloud.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:31.510
Very rarely do we
see organizations

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:35.800
just moving to the cloud without
an aspect of modernization

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:38.710
associated with it.

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:42.400
But any organization
would have to decide

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:45.670
one of these paths
based on the workloads

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:50.810
that they're migrating and the
consumers of those workloads.

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:53.460
Migration is never easy.

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:54.680
It takes time.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:56.765
Sometimes months,
sometimes even years.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:04.260
Irrespective of the path that's
chosen for migration, APIs

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:09.060
and API management can make sure
that the path towards moving

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:11.400
to the cloud is made easier.

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:17.800
Next, let's take a look at
some of the common challenges

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:21.640
that we have seen our customers
face through the various field

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:25.060
engagements we have worked
with the customers globally

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:28.620
or the surveys that
we have conducted.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:33.990
The first and foremost challenge
that any organization faces

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:36.630
is business continuity.

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:41.160
Migrating to the cloud should
not disrupt the existing user

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:44.760
experiences that an
organization has,

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:47.190
and those can be
external experiences

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:49.320
over web or mobile apps.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:52.350
It can be a partner
experience, on even

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:55.710
your internal employee apps.

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:58.740
That's the first challenge.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:02.010
The second is, of
course, security.

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:05.460
When workloads are
migrated to the cloud,

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:09.360
the data invariably
lives in multiple places

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:12.630
and are being accessed
through multiple channels.

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:16.330
Making sure that this
happens in a secure way,

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:18.930
adhering to the compliance
requirement and the best

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:22.220
practices that your organization
has set up is vital.

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:28.630
The cloud challenges visibility.

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:31.450
There are some key questions
that any organization

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:34.600
faces during the
migration to the cloud.

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:37.300
How's the traction
of my migration?

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:39.950
Which workloads have
migrated to the cloud?

00:06:39.950 --> 00:06:42.450
Which are still on premise?

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:47.260
Are my on premise apps still
getting traffic from consumers,

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:51.160
or can I deprecate them
or decommission them?

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:54.490
Answers to these questions
are key to take some business

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:57.130
decisions in any organization.

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:03.420
The fourth challenge
is interoperability.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:06.540
The new or modern apps
which have been replatformed

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:09.150
and move to the cloud
many times don't

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:13.410
want to access the legacy
protocols that the legacy

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:16.560
apps might expose.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:20.940
Also, the onus of talking to
a different kind of back end,

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:23.820
whether it's modern
or legacy, should not

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:25.620
lie with the app developer.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:29.490
The app developer in this
case are our consumers.

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:32.130
Because this has made
their apps more complex,

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:34.320
they will have to write
conditional code based

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:37.290
on whether they are connecting
to a modern app versus a legacy

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:39.070
app.

00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:41.690
So that's the fourth challenge.

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:45.210
The last challenge is
unmanaged services.

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:49.210
Moving to the cloud
invariably results into,

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:51.810
or most of the time results
into a microservices

00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:53.620
based architecture.

00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:56.130
While we understand the
value of microservices

00:07:56.130 --> 00:08:00.660
based architecture, there might
be unmanaged microservices,

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:04.860
which can cause security
challenges, duplication

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:10.200
of code, or
unintended behaviors.

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:12.870
With me so far?

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:15.170
Good.

00:08:15.170 --> 00:08:19.970
Introducing Apigee-- Apigee is
a full lifecycle API management

00:08:19.970 --> 00:08:21.600
platform.

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:24.320
I can talk about Apigee
for the next few hours,

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:26.580
but for the purpose
of this section,

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:28.190
I'm going to just
restrict myself

00:08:28.190 --> 00:08:31.130
to the features of
Apigee that will help us

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:33.409
in mitigating the risks
that we spoke about

00:08:33.409 --> 00:08:35.150
in the previous slide.

00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:37.130
Josh in his section
is going to cover

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:39.320
some of the high-level
key features

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:44.110
that Woolworths is leveraging
of the platform today.

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:46.470
So here, on the
left-hand side, you

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:49.530
see the various end
user experiences,

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:53.770
which are exposed through
web or mobile channels.

00:08:53.770 --> 00:08:57.000
These are your end customers.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:00.030
On the right, you'll
see the back ends

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:02.250
or the system of records.

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:04.920
These system of records
can be on microservices.

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:06.420
It can be a Node.js app.

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:10.170
It can be an app on
GKE or even a legacy

00:09:10.170 --> 00:09:12.128
monolithic application.

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:19.090
The biggest challenge that
any app migration faces

00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:22.960
is the tight coupling
of the user experience

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:27.640
with the system of records,
or the exposure layer

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:30.230
and the consumption layer.

00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:34.100
So Apigee provides a layer of
abstraction between these two

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:38.720
worlds, ensuring service
connectivity, and making sure

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:41.600
that the apps, whether
they are the front end

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:43.940
workloads or the
back end workloads,

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:47.780
can be migrated without
disrupting the existing user

00:09:47.780 --> 00:09:50.562
experiences.

00:09:50.562 --> 00:09:55.180
We spoke about the
security challenge.

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:58.570
Apigee can act both as
a security mediation

00:09:58.570 --> 00:10:02.900
and a security
enforcement clear.

00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:06.730
So as apps are replatformed
and modernized,

00:10:06.730 --> 00:10:09.010
the protocols and the
security mechanisms

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:13.390
that they leverage might be
different than the legacy ones.

00:10:13.390 --> 00:10:17.410
The modern apps might use
protocols or mechanisms,

00:10:17.410 --> 00:10:20.980
like JAR token or OAuth,
and the legacy apps

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:24.580
might be leveraging basic
authentication or SAML.

00:10:24.580 --> 00:10:28.870
Apigee provides uniformity
by exposing a single security

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:33.030
mechanism to the
apps via mediating

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:38.140
the credentials between the
legacy and the modern apps.

00:10:38.140 --> 00:10:42.250
Apigee can also provide
security checks or enforcements,

00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:48.650
like regex checks or data
loss prevention checks

00:10:48.650 --> 00:10:52.030
when the on premise workloads
are calling workloads

00:10:52.030 --> 00:10:52.630
on the cloud.

00:10:56.020 --> 00:10:59.140
The third aspect was visibility.

00:10:59.140 --> 00:11:03.970
Because here Apigee acts as a
facade between the on premises

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:08.350
and the cloud workloads, it
has the required visibility

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:12.160
on how the migration
is progressing.

00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:14.410
What's the traction
on migration?

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:17.920
And it can give us
the data, like which

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:20.080
are the APIs or
API products that

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:22.180
are leveraging newer back ends?

00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:25.720
Which are the ones who are
leveraging legacy back ends?

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:28.180
Have all the traffic
moved to the new back ends

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:32.380
so that I can decommission
my legacy applications?

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:35.470
This allows the platform
and the product owners

00:11:35.470 --> 00:11:38.110
to take business-driven
decisions that are

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:39.730
needed for any organization.

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:44.440
The fourth challenge
that we spoke about

00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:47.350
was interoperability.

00:11:47.350 --> 00:11:49.780
The reality is that
not all apps can

00:11:49.780 --> 00:11:52.690
be migrated at once,
which means that there'll

00:11:52.690 --> 00:11:55.840
be apps which have been
replatformed and modernized

00:11:55.840 --> 00:12:00.230
and move to cloud which still
needs to talk to legacy back

00:12:00.230 --> 00:12:01.390
ends.

00:12:01.390 --> 00:12:05.260
Or there might be newer apps
that are being written today,

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:08.200
and they might have
to talk to both legacy

00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:10.300
and the new back ends.

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:13.690
Apigee helps by
providing an API facade

00:12:13.690 --> 00:12:16.450
which can do the
required transformation,

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:18.730
like SOAP to REST.

00:12:18.730 --> 00:12:22.540
And it takes out the necessity
for the app developers

00:12:22.540 --> 00:12:24.940
of modern apps to
have conditional code

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:28.180
or download specific
libraries to talk to,

00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:30.524
let's say, a SOAP back end.

00:12:34.790 --> 00:12:40.180
Moving on, the last one
was service management.

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:43.330
Lift, shift, and
modernize is a common way

00:12:43.330 --> 00:12:48.640
of migrating to the cloud, and
many organizations leverage

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:51.160
microservices,
often on Kubernetes,

00:12:51.160 --> 00:12:52.210
to achieve the same.

00:12:55.660 --> 00:12:58.150
As I said, while we are
aware of the benefits

00:12:58.150 --> 00:13:01.000
of the microservices
driven architecture,

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:03.940
the challenge is an
explosion of microservices

00:13:03.940 --> 00:13:06.070
in an organization.

00:13:06.070 --> 00:13:08.950
We need the right
security and visibility

00:13:08.950 --> 00:13:13.870
to make sure that those
microservices are adopted

00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:17.440
across the organization to
prevent any duplication of code

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:20.980
or security loopholes.

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:24.970
Istio is a common
way to manage this,

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:27.920
which acts as a service mesh.

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:31.250
Apigee adds an additional
API management layer

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:34.970
by providing security
protocols, like OAuth

00:13:34.970 --> 00:13:37.340
or additional analytics.

00:13:37.340 --> 00:13:39.770
For organizations who
are not leveraging

00:13:39.770 --> 00:13:43.110
Istio or microgateway,
Apigee's microgateway

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:46.990
provides similar capabilities.

00:13:46.990 --> 00:13:50.650
With that, I come to the
end of the first section.

00:13:50.650 --> 00:13:54.370
So we spoke about the various
parts towards migration,

00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:57.310
the top five challenges
that organizations

00:13:57.310 --> 00:14:02.630
face on modernizing and
migrating to the cloud,

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:06.820
and finally, we spoke about
how can APIs and API management

00:14:06.820 --> 00:14:09.940
help mitigate some
of those risks?

00:14:09.940 --> 00:14:12.000
Now, before handing
over to Josh,

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:16.340
I'm going to give you the first
Aussie fun fact of the day.

00:14:16.340 --> 00:14:18.680
Australia is an island nation.

00:14:18.680 --> 00:14:22.160
It has more than 10,000 beaches.

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:26.500
So if you were to visit
one new beach every day,

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:30.670
it's going to take you 27
years to visit all of them.

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:33.090
Who wants to get started?

00:14:33.090 --> 00:14:34.009
Thank you.

00:14:34.009 --> 00:14:35.342
JOSHUA ROGERS: Thank you, Parna.

00:14:35.342 --> 00:14:38.778
[APPLAUSE]

00:14:39.278 --> 00:14:41.772
Thanks.

00:14:41.772 --> 00:14:44.230
All right, so now I'm going to
introduce you to Woolworths.

00:14:44.230 --> 00:14:46.840
Woolworths is a
customer of apologies.

00:14:46.840 --> 00:14:51.640
Woolworths was founded
in Australia in 1924.

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:57.490
They started as a small location
called Woolworths Stupendous

00:14:57.490 --> 00:14:59.240
Bargain Basement.

00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:02.230
And as they continued
to grow and spread

00:15:02.230 --> 00:15:06.670
throughout Australia, they
developed a nationwide presence

00:15:06.670 --> 00:15:08.890
around 1960.

00:15:08.890 --> 00:15:12.220
They were soon on their way
to becoming a household name.

00:15:12.220 --> 00:15:15.490
Come February 1998, they
launched Home Shop, which

00:15:15.490 --> 00:15:18.010
is e-commerce for groceries.

00:15:18.010 --> 00:15:21.910
And it was one of the
first in the world

00:15:21.910 --> 00:15:26.622
to offer groceries fresh to
your door after ordering online.

00:15:26.622 --> 00:15:28.330
There was a couple
other group businesses

00:15:28.330 --> 00:15:31.270
who also endeavored online
during this time as well.

00:15:31.270 --> 00:15:35.350
Woolworths is a combination of
a lot of smaller businesses,

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:39.490
and each individual business
had a concentrated effort

00:15:39.490 --> 00:15:44.630
to establish an online
presence individually.

00:15:44.630 --> 00:15:46.640
So as these businesses
continued to evolve

00:15:46.640 --> 00:15:49.042
and new technology
becomes available,

00:15:49.042 --> 00:15:51.500
our websites that were once
hosted in a private data center

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:53.990
were lacking access
to the technologies,

00:15:53.990 --> 00:15:56.450
and we were fastly
getting outpaced.

00:15:56.450 --> 00:15:58.910
So with that developed
an opportunity for us

00:15:58.910 --> 00:16:01.335
to migrate into the cloud.

00:16:01.335 --> 00:16:02.960
Not only did we want
to lift and shift,

00:16:02.960 --> 00:16:05.180
like Parna had spoken
to before, but we also

00:16:05.180 --> 00:16:06.800
wanted to modernize it.

00:16:06.800 --> 00:16:10.250
And it's not your regular
digital transformation.

00:16:10.250 --> 00:16:13.880
It's also a supermarket
at nature trying

00:16:13.880 --> 00:16:17.320
to enter a digital market.

00:16:17.320 --> 00:16:21.490
So in order for us to accomplish
some of these digital feats,

00:16:21.490 --> 00:16:25.340
the Woolworths group started
combining a few of our efforts

00:16:25.340 --> 00:16:29.080
and establishing a holistic
customer experience

00:16:29.080 --> 00:16:31.580
by way of WooliesX.

00:16:31.580 --> 00:16:34.160
WooliesX is a combination
of engineering and business

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:37.670
professionals that concentrate
on the customer experience

00:16:37.670 --> 00:16:41.210
at the forefront of every
decision that's made.

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:44.360
You can see here we have
over 500 team members and 400

00:16:44.360 --> 00:16:47.270
customer service agents
working to make sure

00:16:47.270 --> 00:16:51.350
that our team understands
the needs of our customers.

00:16:51.350 --> 00:16:54.080
Things that we've been able to
accomplish in a few short years

00:16:54.080 --> 00:16:57.470
since this
organization was formed

00:16:57.470 --> 00:17:02.530
are we are up to 11.5
million rewards members.

00:17:02.530 --> 00:17:06.430
So to give you a little context,
there's only 24 million people

00:17:06.430 --> 00:17:12.970
living in Australia, so we're
nearly half of the population

00:17:12.970 --> 00:17:14.980
is a rewards member.

00:17:14.980 --> 00:17:21.089
Another quick fact is there's
only about 12,000 households,

00:17:21.089 --> 00:17:23.500
or sorry, 12 million
households in Australia,

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:27.485
so we're 1 for 1 in every
household, not particularly

00:17:27.485 --> 00:17:30.700
every household,
but numbers wise.

00:17:30.700 --> 00:17:33.010
Some other things to note--
we do over $2 billion

00:17:33.010 --> 00:17:35.380
in transactions.

00:17:35.380 --> 00:17:37.373
And that's quite
a big number when

00:17:37.373 --> 00:17:39.040
we're talking about
a supermarket that's

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:39.640
moving online.

00:17:43.070 --> 00:17:45.490
So how did we get
started on Apigee?

00:17:45.490 --> 00:17:48.880
We had the need to connect
our consumer applications

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:51.610
with some back end services
that were not particularly owned

00:17:51.610 --> 00:17:52.960
by us.

00:17:52.960 --> 00:17:54.880
We had applications
that need to connect

00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:57.790
to third-party
services, and we also

00:17:57.790 --> 00:18:02.060
had employee apps that need
to connect to our HR services.

00:18:02.060 --> 00:18:08.580
We started with two businesses,
six developers, built 15 APIs,

00:18:08.580 --> 00:18:11.010
and was handling around 45
transactions per second.

00:18:13.880 --> 00:18:17.055
Apigee helped to streamline
the developer experience.

00:18:17.055 --> 00:18:18.680
A couple of the key
features that Parna

00:18:18.680 --> 00:18:22.430
wasn't able to speak into
are the Developer Portal.

00:18:22.430 --> 00:18:25.730
The Developer Portal allows
you to create an API key

00:18:25.730 --> 00:18:27.110
and understand
the contracts that

00:18:27.110 --> 00:18:30.380
are established between our
APIs and you as the consumer.

00:18:30.380 --> 00:18:33.000
The consumer doesn't have
to be an internal employee,

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:34.640
it can also be an
external consumer.

00:18:34.640 --> 00:18:36.140
And that's one of
the things that we

00:18:36.140 --> 00:18:37.970
want to make sure
that people understand

00:18:37.970 --> 00:18:40.880
is anything that's good enough
for your internal teams should

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:44.000
be provided as good or even
better for your external.

00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:45.440
And similarly
anything that you're

00:18:45.440 --> 00:18:49.063
going to deliver high quality
for your external consumers,

00:18:49.063 --> 00:18:51.230
you should treat your
developers with the same level

00:18:51.230 --> 00:18:54.040
of establishment.

00:18:54.040 --> 00:18:57.250
Some other things that really
benefit us are permissions.

00:18:57.250 --> 00:18:59.890
The granularity of permissions
on the Apigee platform

00:18:59.890 --> 00:19:04.870
allow us to get down to
delivery of the application

00:19:04.870 --> 00:19:08.590
to production, traceability
of those transactions

00:19:08.590 --> 00:19:13.210
during live messaging,
as well as visibility

00:19:13.210 --> 00:19:18.710
into analytics and controls
that help safeguard our APIs.

00:19:18.710 --> 00:19:23.240
API abuse prevention in
the way of Apigee's sense

00:19:23.240 --> 00:19:27.590
allows us to capitalize on
Apigee's AI and ML models

00:19:27.590 --> 00:19:33.050
to identify anomalies and
any other malicious behavior,

00:19:33.050 --> 00:19:35.638
and stop it before it
gets to our systems.

00:19:35.638 --> 00:19:37.430
And this is a key thing
for us to make sure

00:19:37.430 --> 00:19:39.680
that our customers
themselves are getting

00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:44.360
a consistent experience, no
matter who is being malicious

00:19:44.360 --> 00:19:46.380
on our network.

00:19:46.380 --> 00:19:48.590
And additionally,
API Monitoring allows

00:19:48.590 --> 00:19:52.010
us to make sure that
our traffic patterns are

00:19:52.010 --> 00:19:53.870
where we expect them to be.

00:19:53.870 --> 00:19:57.050
If we see an increase of
500s over a period of time,

00:19:57.050 --> 00:20:00.170
we can alert ourselves
over email, Slack,

00:20:00.170 --> 00:20:02.330
or even a customized web hook.

00:20:02.330 --> 00:20:04.640
And those web hooks can be
very powerful to integrate

00:20:04.640 --> 00:20:07.265
with any of the systems that are
not on boarded into the Apigee

00:20:07.265 --> 00:20:08.750
platform.

00:20:08.750 --> 00:20:10.880
One of the other key things
that I want to mention

00:20:10.880 --> 00:20:13.750
is Parna, as well as
other of our accounts

00:20:13.750 --> 00:20:16.580
and support engagements at
Apigee, have been along with us

00:20:16.580 --> 00:20:19.130
every step of the way and have
been critical for our success

00:20:19.130 --> 00:20:19.797
on the platform.

00:20:22.350 --> 00:20:25.410
So with our success, we unlock
new business opportunities.

00:20:25.410 --> 00:20:27.900
Some of these are
business ventures

00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:31.200
that capitalize on
just an abstraction

00:20:31.200 --> 00:20:33.450
between the customer and
our back end services

00:20:33.450 --> 00:20:36.360
and don't offer anything
in the way of really

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:41.790
adopting or spreading
data between the services.

00:20:41.790 --> 00:20:44.250
Other services are
fully connected

00:20:44.250 --> 00:20:47.170
into each other via data.

00:20:47.170 --> 00:20:50.190
So our insurance
service, our credit cards

00:20:50.190 --> 00:20:53.070
and financial services
and gift cards, those

00:20:53.070 --> 00:20:54.690
are all services
that are brand new

00:20:54.690 --> 00:20:57.962
and we're trying to
explore as a company.

00:20:57.962 --> 00:20:59.670
Later on, we'll build
up the technologies

00:20:59.670 --> 00:21:00.900
to establish them ourselves.

00:21:00.900 --> 00:21:03.180
But the presence on
Apigee will not change.

00:21:03.180 --> 00:21:06.760
We'll try and keep that contract
as consistent as possible.

00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:09.300
Scan and Go, which is a
new, innovative customer

00:21:09.300 --> 00:21:10.950
experience for
our stores, allows

00:21:10.950 --> 00:21:13.812
customers to walk in,
find the goods they need,

00:21:13.812 --> 00:21:16.020
scan them with their phone,
and complete the checkout

00:21:16.020 --> 00:21:17.687
process themselves.

00:21:17.687 --> 00:21:19.770
They don't have to walk
through our checkout lines

00:21:19.770 --> 00:21:22.120
or deal with any
of our employees.

00:21:22.120 --> 00:21:27.150
They can just create their own
experience within our stores.

00:21:27.150 --> 00:21:31.230
Woolworths online also reaches
almost half a million customers

00:21:31.230 --> 00:21:32.460
actively.

00:21:32.460 --> 00:21:34.980
And we run our mobile
applications through Apigee

00:21:34.980 --> 00:21:36.780
to make sure that,
again, our customer's

00:21:36.780 --> 00:21:39.060
getting a consistent
experience when

00:21:39.060 --> 00:21:41.470
integrating with our services.

00:21:41.470 --> 00:21:44.140
And then down on the bottom
right, you see rewards.

00:21:44.140 --> 00:21:46.780
Rewards is a critical component
to collecting that customer

00:21:46.780 --> 00:21:49.930
experience data and making sure
that we can consume it and then

00:21:49.930 --> 00:21:51.440
respond to it.

00:21:51.440 --> 00:21:54.820
We can take the rewards
data and integrate it

00:21:54.820 --> 00:21:57.100
with every single one
of our services because

00:21:57.100 --> 00:22:00.750
of the availability
that we have on Apigee.

00:22:00.750 --> 00:22:03.060
So we continue to grow.

00:22:03.060 --> 00:22:06.210
We now have reached over
10 businesses internally.

00:22:06.210 --> 00:22:08.760
We have more than 18
developers actively developing

00:22:08.760 --> 00:22:14.460
on APIs, the evolution,
the modernization,

00:22:14.460 --> 00:22:16.230
the governance of those APIs.

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:17.730
We're trying to
keep them up to date

00:22:17.730 --> 00:22:22.230
and making sure that all
1,000 of our available APIs

00:22:22.230 --> 00:22:25.430
are up to the same
standard as we want.

00:22:25.430 --> 00:22:27.730
And now we transact
over 480 times

00:22:27.730 --> 00:22:30.670
a second on all of our APIs.

00:22:30.670 --> 00:22:35.380
And that's something that we've
peaked quite a ways higher,

00:22:35.380 --> 00:22:40.500
but it is without a hitch.

00:22:40.500 --> 00:22:44.130
So as with most growth, there
becomes a few challenges.

00:22:44.130 --> 00:22:45.210
There's an evolution.

00:22:45.210 --> 00:22:46.775
New opportunities arise.

00:22:46.775 --> 00:22:48.900
And I'll talk to you about
one of our opportunities

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:54.270
to migrate from a database as
a service to GCP Cloud SQL.

00:22:54.270 --> 00:22:56.190
All of our back end
ancillary systems

00:22:56.190 --> 00:23:01.290
deliver a large parcel of
data into a blob storage,

00:23:01.290 --> 00:23:05.460
and then we utilize triggers
to transact that data

00:23:05.460 --> 00:23:08.430
and load it into our databases.

00:23:08.430 --> 00:23:11.100
Apigee acts as an
abstraction layer.

00:23:11.100 --> 00:23:12.960
So during the development
of Scan and Go,

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:15.240
our product that I
was telling you about,

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:19.980
we needed to move the data from
our database as the service

00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:21.882
over to Cloud SQL.

00:23:21.882 --> 00:23:24.090
Our database as a service
was operating on an account

00:23:24.090 --> 00:23:26.100
that we didn't have
full control over.

00:23:26.100 --> 00:23:28.470
We didn't have the
ability to react

00:23:28.470 --> 00:23:31.780
or permiss any
additional resources.

00:23:31.780 --> 00:23:34.380
So in order for us to
continue to grow and expand

00:23:34.380 --> 00:23:39.130
our operations, we need to take
that control into ourselves.

00:23:39.130 --> 00:23:43.540
So we utilized Apigee to
continue that contract

00:23:43.540 --> 00:23:47.540
to our customers while we
make those back end changes.

00:23:47.540 --> 00:23:50.950
So a lot of that learning
that we had to do

00:23:50.950 --> 00:23:54.250
is still with a very
new organization.

00:23:54.250 --> 00:23:57.410
We're only a year and a
half in at this point.

00:23:57.410 --> 00:23:59.770
And we were able to do it
with the support of Parna

00:23:59.770 --> 00:24:02.980
and the team at Apigee
to redevelop our Lotus

00:24:02.980 --> 00:24:04.930
scripts within Google.

00:24:04.930 --> 00:24:08.440
We were able to move the
data over preemptively.

00:24:08.440 --> 00:24:11.163
We're also able to test and go
through all of the scenarios

00:24:11.163 --> 00:24:12.580
to make sure that
our applications

00:24:12.580 --> 00:24:15.250
are going to respond
to the new data store.

00:24:15.250 --> 00:24:18.520
And we were able to do it
with only a 30 minute window

00:24:18.520 --> 00:24:21.070
of execution.

00:24:21.070 --> 00:24:25.370
Special thanks to Terry,
Shyam, Madhan, our API team,

00:24:25.370 --> 00:24:28.930
our business teams, are
QAs, and the support staff

00:24:28.930 --> 00:24:31.570
who made sure that
we were diligent

00:24:31.570 --> 00:24:33.310
about every aspect
of this migration.

00:24:36.100 --> 00:24:39.260
So now, we'll transition
to another Aussie fun fact.

00:24:39.260 --> 00:24:43.530
And we've added on this slide
the crest from Australia.

00:24:43.530 --> 00:24:47.790
A good fact about the kangaroo
and the emu are they cannot

00:24:47.790 --> 00:24:48.850
work backwards.

00:24:48.850 --> 00:24:51.450
And so there's a bit of
meaning as to why they

00:24:51.450 --> 00:24:52.860
face each other on the crest.

00:24:56.430 --> 00:24:59.580
So now I'd like to
introduce you to Olive.

00:24:59.580 --> 00:25:02.270
Olive is our virtual
assistant that we're

00:25:02.270 --> 00:25:05.030
