00:33:29.560 --> 00:33:31.930
to make it available
for our customers

00:33:31.930 --> 00:33:33.700
to shop or interact
with our services

00:33:33.700 --> 00:33:35.210
on a variety of platforms.

00:33:35.210 --> 00:33:38.440
But then the long-term increase
of maintaining that customer

00:33:38.440 --> 00:33:40.878
retention is also beneficial.

00:33:40.878 --> 00:33:41.920
PARNA BHATTACHARYA: Sure.

00:33:41.920 --> 00:33:43.540
Thanks, Josh.

00:33:43.540 --> 00:33:47.560
The next one, again, for you,
somebody from Delta Airlines,

00:33:47.560 --> 00:33:49.360
again, upvoted a few times.

00:33:49.360 --> 00:33:51.610
Could you talk about
the API development

00:33:51.610 --> 00:33:54.130
roadmap used in WooliesX?

00:33:54.130 --> 00:33:56.530
How the APIs were identified?

00:33:56.530 --> 00:33:58.690
Was that a business
driven top down?

00:33:58.690 --> 00:34:01.900
Was it a project driven
bottom up approach

00:34:01.900 --> 00:34:03.717
to develop and deliver APIs?

00:34:03.717 --> 00:34:05.050
JOSHUA ROGERS: Yeah, absolutely.

00:34:05.050 --> 00:34:08.788
So the necessity for
data to become available

00:34:08.788 --> 00:34:11.080
should be the first thing
that you consider when you're

00:34:11.080 --> 00:34:12.190
developing your services.

00:34:12.190 --> 00:34:15.409
It's not necessarily the
data that's valuable to you,

00:34:15.409 --> 00:34:19.130
but the data available for other
services to integrate with you.

00:34:19.130 --> 00:34:22.150
Especially in a distributed
microservice world,

00:34:22.150 --> 00:34:23.739
you want to make
sure that you're not

00:34:23.739 --> 00:34:25.960
having to modify the
contracts to provide

00:34:25.960 --> 00:34:28.300
the data that somebody might
need to make a business

00:34:28.300 --> 00:34:30.580
transaction possible.

00:34:30.580 --> 00:34:32.080
One of the situations
that we've ran

00:34:32.080 --> 00:34:36.790
into while establishing
a virtual assistant

00:34:36.790 --> 00:34:39.250
was we didn't necessarily
have access to the data

00:34:39.250 --> 00:34:42.370
that we wanted to improve
the customer experience,

00:34:42.370 --> 00:34:45.580
things like, what time
will my order be delivered?

00:34:45.580 --> 00:34:49.480
So we're constantly working
on enabling integration

00:34:49.480 --> 00:34:50.943
and putting that
at the forefront

00:34:50.943 --> 00:34:52.360
of a lot of the
business decisions

00:34:52.360 --> 00:34:57.260
that we make when first
designing those services.

00:34:57.260 --> 00:34:59.760
PARNA BHATTACHARYA: Yeah, it's
always a combination of both,

00:34:59.760 --> 00:35:04.340
but understanding who are
the consumers of your APIs,

00:35:04.340 --> 00:35:06.890
and making sure that you
identify and then work

00:35:06.890 --> 00:35:10.520
backwards from there is
usually a good recipe.

00:35:10.520 --> 00:35:12.560
JOSHUA ROGERS: Yeah, absolutely.

00:35:12.560 --> 00:35:15.000
PARNA BHATTACHARYA: The next
one from Optimals, again,

00:35:15.000 --> 00:35:16.880
a lot of upvotes for that.

00:35:16.880 --> 00:35:19.550
How do you data
mine bad consumers?

00:35:19.550 --> 00:35:22.580
What advice would you give to
a government entity looking

00:35:22.580 --> 00:35:25.590
to leverage Apigee?

00:35:25.590 --> 00:35:28.340
First of all, leverage
Apigee, absolutely.

00:35:28.340 --> 00:35:31.440
And if you have questions, come
to the Apigee booth downstairs.

00:35:31.440 --> 00:35:33.960
But handing it over to Josh.

00:35:33.960 --> 00:35:35.850
How to data mine bad consumers.

00:35:37.567 --> 00:35:39.900
JOSHUA ROGERS: So you repeat
the question one more time?

00:35:39.900 --> 00:35:42.192
PARNA BHATTACHARYA: How do
the data mine bad consumers?

00:35:42.192 --> 00:35:45.130
I guess this is from an
external end user perspective,

00:35:45.130 --> 00:35:47.900
from our Sense, I
guess, about detection.

00:35:47.900 --> 00:35:50.210
JOSHUA ROGERS: Right, so
Apigee Sense provides us

00:35:50.210 --> 00:35:56.640
a bit of the bad actor
detection, and we're actually--

00:35:56.640 --> 00:35:59.130
it doesn't ever reach
our systems themselves.

00:35:59.130 --> 00:36:01.800
We're able to get a report
on what transactions

00:36:01.800 --> 00:36:06.480
have been blocked on the Apigee
layer, and then react to those.

00:36:06.480 --> 00:36:08.400
If we wanted to
try and data mine

00:36:08.400 --> 00:36:12.420
to make sure that we're
analyzing the traffic

00:36:12.420 --> 00:36:16.140
patterns or the opportunities
that people potentially

00:36:16.140 --> 00:36:19.380
might explore with other
APIs that don't process

00:36:19.380 --> 00:36:21.780
through Apigee, there
are opportunities for us

00:36:21.780 --> 00:36:25.710
to go back and analyze those
reports to see which ones are

00:36:25.710 --> 00:36:29.220
actively being
actioned, as well as

00:36:29.220 --> 00:36:31.830
any of the traffic that
comes all the way through.

00:36:31.830 --> 00:36:33.690
Apigee provides an
opportunity for us

00:36:33.690 --> 00:36:37.740
to analyze the data themselves,
or sorry, data from those

00:36:37.740 --> 00:36:38.860
transactions as well.

00:36:38.860 --> 00:36:41.880
So if we do see something that
may have made it through Apigee

00:36:41.880 --> 00:36:44.610
Sense, we're still able to
go back and try and identify

00:36:44.610 --> 00:36:47.250
some of those traffic
patterns and utilize

00:36:47.250 --> 00:36:49.033
our own defenses at that point.

00:36:49.033 --> 00:36:50.700
PARNA BHATTACHARYA:
Yeah, and especially

00:36:50.700 --> 00:36:52.117
in the case of
Woolworths, we have

00:36:52.117 --> 00:36:55.170
seen how geolocation, there
is the originating traffic?

00:36:55.170 --> 00:36:56.520
That has helped quite a bit.

00:36:56.520 --> 00:36:59.730
Like, you don't expect
people from Asian countries

00:36:59.730 --> 00:37:03.480
to put online requests on
Woolworths in Australia.

00:37:03.480 --> 00:37:06.150
So geolocation, again,
a part of Apigee Sense,

00:37:06.150 --> 00:37:09.250
has helped quite a bit.

00:37:09.250 --> 00:37:11.640
And again, we do have a
lot of government entities

00:37:11.640 --> 00:37:13.650
who are Apigee's customers.

00:37:13.650 --> 00:37:17.740
A lot of security patterns that
we have been able to establish,

00:37:17.740 --> 00:37:20.300
so definitely worth
a chat to whoever

00:37:20.300 --> 00:37:23.040
has posted that question.

00:37:23.040 --> 00:37:24.630
Quickly moving on,
we do have time,

00:37:24.630 --> 00:37:26.462
so I guess we are doing fine.

00:37:30.190 --> 00:37:33.670
In your experience, what are
your most critical patterns

00:37:33.670 --> 00:37:37.808
and antipatterns for
API driven development?

00:37:37.808 --> 00:37:40.350
JOSHUA ROGERS: Yeah, I think I
touched on that a bit earlier.

00:37:40.350 --> 00:37:42.570
And you never know
the data that's

00:37:42.570 --> 00:37:45.210
going to be consumed
until you go to utilize it

00:37:45.210 --> 00:37:46.625
and it's not there.

00:37:46.625 --> 00:37:48.000
You want to make
sure that you're

00:37:48.000 --> 00:37:52.350
trying to identify the best ways
to vent the data in a protected

00:37:52.350 --> 00:37:56.250
manner, but make it available
for internal consumers,

00:37:56.250 --> 00:38:00.450
and in some cases, external
consumers to identify.

00:38:00.450 --> 00:38:03.870
The other thing is to make
sure that your developers know

00:38:03.870 --> 00:38:06.390
very well how to version.

00:38:06.390 --> 00:38:11.350
An API forms a contract
between consumers and services.

00:38:11.350 --> 00:38:14.010
And if we don't
version it properly,

00:38:14.010 --> 00:38:16.890
features or data might not
be available the next time

00:38:16.890 --> 00:38:18.310
they call your service.

00:38:18.310 --> 00:38:21.900
And we want to make
sure that, internally,

00:38:21.900 --> 00:38:25.380
every contract that
we commit to maintains

00:38:25.380 --> 00:38:31.560
the same level of inputs,
outputs, and data formats as we

00:38:31.560 --> 00:38:34.590
once committed to all the
way through the lifecycle.

00:38:34.590 --> 00:38:36.040
Which leads me to
the next point,

00:38:36.040 --> 00:38:41.130
which is know and establish
a deprecation policy.

00:38:41.130 --> 00:38:43.920
Make sure that before you put
something into production,

00:38:43.920 --> 00:38:46.440
you know exactly how you're
going to go about taking it out

00:38:46.440 --> 00:38:50.460
of production, as well, when
the validity or lifecycle

00:38:50.460 --> 00:38:52.130
of that service is over.

00:38:52.130 --> 00:38:54.380
I think those would be the
three things that I would--

00:38:54.380 --> 00:38:55.672
PARNA BHATTACHARYA: Absolutely.

00:38:55.672 --> 00:38:59.370
And again, Apigee has
published a book recently,

00:38:59.370 --> 00:39:03.120
and it's technology
agnostics on antipatterns

00:39:03.120 --> 00:39:05.190
and best practices around APIs.

00:39:05.190 --> 00:39:11.070
So maybe I'll put a link into
