00:41:24.760 --> 00:41:27.580
JOSHUA ROGERS: --web hooks, as
well as pager duty integration

00:41:27.580 --> 00:41:28.640
natively.

00:41:28.640 --> 00:41:32.840
So you can work your
alerts into those systems.

00:41:32.840 --> 00:41:34.810
Things that I would
utilize web hooks for

00:41:34.810 --> 00:41:38.830
would be executing maybe
a service Wow incident

00:41:38.830 --> 00:41:40.660
or some other
incident managements

00:41:40.660 --> 00:41:44.320
to try and get you a bit more
of a record on your incident

00:41:44.320 --> 00:41:47.448
management systems that
Apigee can tie into.

00:41:47.448 --> 00:41:48.490
PARNA BHATTACHARYA: Sure.

00:41:51.290 --> 00:41:54.980
Another question from
Delta Airlines Rajeev.

00:41:54.980 --> 00:41:58.730
Was your API development
centralized or distributed?

00:41:58.730 --> 00:42:00.670
A great question again.

00:42:00.670 --> 00:42:03.010
How did the org adopt to APIs?

00:42:03.010 --> 00:42:04.900
Was there a separate
governance team

00:42:04.900 --> 00:42:08.110
to build and enforce standards?

00:42:08.110 --> 00:42:11.850
JOSHUA ROGERS: So the
question around centralization

00:42:11.850 --> 00:42:13.650
versus distribution--

00:42:13.650 --> 00:42:17.250
we utilized a central
team structure.

00:42:17.250 --> 00:42:19.800
And my developers
themselves are actually

00:42:19.800 --> 00:42:21.570
integrated into the squads.

00:42:21.570 --> 00:42:26.540
So we utilized the central team
to form a community around APIs

00:42:26.540 --> 00:42:29.490
and best practices,
governance, and then

00:42:29.490 --> 00:42:32.610
also to share knowledge
around what has worked

00:42:32.610 --> 00:42:34.420
and what has caused challenges.

00:42:34.420 --> 00:42:36.210
But the developers
themselves actually

00:42:36.210 --> 00:42:38.140
operate strictly
within the businesses,

00:42:38.140 --> 00:42:41.178
so it is quite the decentralized
model in that sense.

00:42:41.178 --> 00:42:42.220
PARNA BHATTACHARYA: Yeah.

00:42:42.220 --> 00:42:45.330
And usually, our recommendation
when we work with customers

00:42:45.330 --> 00:42:47.850
is to start maybe with
a centralized team

00:42:47.850 --> 00:42:51.060
to ensure that the best
practices and the governance

00:42:51.060 --> 00:42:52.680
is being put in place.

00:42:52.680 --> 00:42:57.150
And then as you mature and
the API program matures,

00:42:57.150 --> 00:43:00.090
it makes sense to have
a decentralized team.

00:43:00.090 --> 00:43:02.610
And we call it governance
with a small g, which

00:43:02.610 --> 00:43:05.760
means that the idea
is to enable the team

00:43:05.760 --> 00:43:08.040
to innovate and
leverage the platform,

00:43:08.040 --> 00:43:12.570
rather than act as
another gate there.

00:43:12.570 --> 00:43:13.508
But, yes, absolutely.

00:43:13.508 --> 00:43:16.050
I think we have seen multiple
customers follow the same route

00:43:16.050 --> 00:43:17.425
that Woolworths
has done through.

00:43:21.230 --> 00:43:24.010
Can you speak to Apigee
deployment model in use

00:43:24.010 --> 00:43:26.200
at Woolworths?

00:43:26.200 --> 00:43:29.930
Are you using Apache
cloud on prem or hybrid?

00:43:29.930 --> 00:43:32.410
JOSHUA ROGERS: So right
now, we operate solely

00:43:32.410 --> 00:43:36.310
on cloud services within Apigee.

00:43:36.310 --> 00:43:39.340
That said, we are working with
the engineering teams at Apigee

00:43:39.340 --> 00:43:42.610
to be one of the early
adopters of the Hybrid Cloud,

00:43:42.610 --> 00:43:48.310
looking to see how it fits into
our current cloud operations,

00:43:48.310 --> 00:43:51.340
and permissions
models, and structures.

00:43:51.340 --> 00:43:54.880
So the way that we organize
our services within GCP

00:43:54.880 --> 00:43:56.440
is going to be
different than the way

00:43:56.440 --> 00:43:58.000
that we organize
with within Azure,

00:43:58.000 --> 00:44:00.250
and we want to work
with Apigee to make sure

00:44:00.250 --> 00:44:02.620
that it's comfortable
when we move Apigee

00:44:02.620 --> 00:44:05.450
into those different
cloud providers.

00:44:05.450 --> 00:44:11.200
And so I would say that
primarily on cloud,

00:44:11.200 --> 00:44:13.582
we utilize the tools
that are provided for us.

00:44:13.582 --> 00:44:15.040
And then in the
future, we're going

00:44:15.040 --> 00:44:17.560
to be utilizing more
of a CI/CD delivery

00:44:17.560 --> 00:44:22.480
model to each individual pod
that we deploy for Apigee.

00:44:22.480 --> 00:44:25.030
PARNA BHATTACHARYA: Sure.

00:44:25.030 --> 00:44:27.150
Another question
on, can you let us

00:44:27.150 --> 00:44:31.290
know what sort of API
versioning features

00:44:31.290 --> 00:44:33.900
are provided and leveraged?

00:44:33.900 --> 00:44:35.947
So this is part of the
API design, it says.

00:44:35.947 --> 00:44:36.780
JOSHUA ROGERS: Yeah.

00:44:36.780 --> 00:44:39.300
And what Apigee
provides you with

00:44:39.300 --> 00:44:42.600
is a few different environments
that you can set up

00:44:42.600 --> 00:44:45.840
to stage any of your
production changes

00:44:45.840 --> 00:44:48.540
before you get them all the
way through your pipelines

00:44:48.540 --> 00:44:49.620
to production.

00:44:49.620 --> 00:44:51.810
Within each stage,
you're getting access

00:44:51.810 --> 00:44:53.550
to every single version.

00:44:53.550 --> 00:44:58.740
A version is actually, in Apigee
Sense, a set of artifacts.

00:44:58.740 --> 00:45:02.010
And once a set of artifacts has
been uploaded to the platform,

00:45:02.010 --> 00:45:04.830
it's available to be made
into a production service

00:45:04.830 --> 00:45:08.890
or made available on any
of those environments.

00:45:08.890 --> 00:45:10.920
And it's very quickly
to demote or promote

00:45:10.920 --> 00:45:13.470
one of those versions
into an operation.

00:45:13.470 --> 00:45:16.290
The actual management of
the versioning of the API

00:45:16.290 --> 00:45:18.450
should be done within
your code, and then

00:45:18.450 --> 00:45:22.860
delivered to the system in a way
that allows you to manage which

00:45:22.860 --> 00:45:25.350
ones are currently in
production without affecting

00:45:25.350 --> 00:45:27.600
any of the other deployments
that you've already made.

00:45:31.215 --> 00:45:33.340
PARNA BHATTACHARYA: Maybe
this is the last question

00:45:33.340 --> 00:45:34.263
that we take now.

00:45:34.263 --> 00:45:36.180
JOSHUA ROGERS: Yeah,
we're running up on time.

00:45:36.180 --> 00:45:37.555
PARNA BHATTACHARYA:
Yeah, we will

00:45:37.555 --> 00:45:39.010
be running out of time soon.

00:45:39.010 --> 00:45:42.400
How do you engage with
external app developers?

00:45:42.400 --> 00:45:46.150
And how did you achieve better
engagement and participation?

00:45:46.150 --> 00:45:49.180
Again, a key part
of any API program

00:45:49.180 --> 00:45:53.735
is engaging the app developers,
both internal and external.

00:45:53.735 --> 00:45:56.110
JOSHUA ROGERS: So each one of
our external app developers

00:45:56.110 --> 00:45:58.390
goes through our
business-to-business team.

00:45:58.390 --> 00:45:59.890
And the
business-to-business team

00:45:59.890 --> 00:46:03.380
has a core function to
establish that relationship.

00:46:03.380 --> 00:46:04.990
But as you can
imagine, the developers

00:46:04.990 --> 00:46:07.390
are not part of that
transaction usually,

00:46:07.390 --> 00:46:10.960
so a lot of our documentation
is available via the developer

00:46:10.960 --> 00:46:14.050
portal for the APIs
that we make available

00:46:14.050 --> 00:46:16.090
for our external consumers.

00:46:16.090 --> 00:46:18.760
The adoption of it,
though, is driven

00:46:18.760 --> 00:46:21.130
by an active conversation
that we maintain

00:46:21.130 --> 00:46:23.350
between our developers,
our external developers

00:46:23.350 --> 00:46:24.550
and our internal developers.

00:46:24.550 --> 00:46:26.740
We provide an
opportunity for them

00:46:26.740 --> 00:46:31.330
to get together and actually
discuss any of the challenges

00:46:31.330 --> 00:46:34.040
that the external consumer
might come across,

00:46:34.040 --> 00:46:37.485
and then also work to make
sure that we address them.

00:46:37.485 --> 00:46:39.860
PARNA BHATTACHARYA: Yeah, and
we are seeing organizations

00:46:39.860 --> 00:46:42.740
have a specific rule
around evangelism,

00:46:42.740 --> 00:46:45.820
both internal and external.

00:46:45.820 --> 00:46:48.200
And hackathons play
an important role

00:46:48.200 --> 00:46:51.320
to make sure that your APIs
are well-known internally,

00:46:51.320 --> 00:46:54.920
and even expose it to
the external consumers.

00:46:54.920 --> 00:46:59.570
So even Slack hackathons
actually help.

00:46:59.570 --> 00:47:03.260
All right, with that, thank
you, again, for staying back.

00:47:03.260 --> 00:47:05.157
Let's head to the afterparty.

00:47:05.157 --> 00:47:05.990
Thank you very much.

00:47:05.990 --> 00:47:08.090
[APPLAUSE]

00:47:08.690 --> 00:47:12.040
[MUSIC PLAYING]

